Understanding the statement - The statement asserts that no matter the shape or size of a triangle, the total measure of all three interior angles will always equal 180°. This is a fundamental property of triangles in Euclidean geometry.

Importance of proof - The fact that this statement has been proven in a previous chapter indicates that it relies on established axioms and theorems. Proving such statements reinforces their validity and allows them to be used in further mathematical reasoning.

Application of the theorem - This theorem can be applied to solve various geometrical problems, such as finding unknown angle measures in triangles, and it serves as a basis for more complex geometric concepts.

Examples:

• Example 1: ( 50° + 60° + 70° = 180° ) - This shows that a triangle with angles of 50°, 60°, and 70° satisfies the theorem.

• Example 2: ( 30° + 90° + 60° = 180° ) - Here, the angles of a right triangle also add up to 180°, confirming the theorem holds true.

• Example 3: A triangle with angles ( x ), ( y ), and ( z ) can be expressed as ( x + y + z = 180° ). This equation can be used to find missing angle measures when one or more angles are known.

Summary: The statement regarding the sum of the interior angles of a triangle being 180° is a fundamental truth in geometry, supported by proof and essential for solving various mathematical problems. ✨

